  "position": 5,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Problems involving congruence and similarity",
  "summary": "Model maps, plans, photographs, shadows and indirect measurements using congruence or similarity.",
  "outcomes": [
    "2.5"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g2-lesson-04"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Translate a real context into a scale or proportion.",
    "Convert units before using a map or model scale.",
    "Check whether length or area is being scaled."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g2-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-25-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Identify which measurements correspond, convert them to compatible units, and write a proportion. For shadows and indirect measurement, equal angles create similar triangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\frac{\\text{height}_1}{\\text{shadow}_1}=\\frac{\\text{height}_2}{\\text{shadow}_2}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-05-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Scale drawings, maps, models and shadows are applications of similarity. The diagram shows a pole and a tree on level ground at the same time of day. The sun's rays make equal angles with the ground, and both uprights are at right angles to it, so the two triangles are similar: height ÷ shadow is the same for both. A scale 1 : n means that one unit on a plan or model corresponds to n of the same units in reality."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g2-lesson-05-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-05-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Decide the direction: actual length=plan length×n, while plan length=actual length÷n. Areas use n². In nested scale problems, multiply successive linear factors first, then square only if the requested quantity is an area."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-25-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: shadows",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the diagram, a 1.8 m pole casts a 2.4 m shadow, and at the same time a tree casts a 14 m shadow. Find the height h of the tree."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The triangles are similar, so height ÷ shadow is equal."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\frac{1.8}{2.4}=\\frac{h}{14}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "h=14\\times\\frac{1.8}{2.4}=14\\times0.75=10.5\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The tree's shadow is 14 ÷ 2.4 ≈ 5.8 times the pole's, and 10.5 ÷ 1.8 ≈ 5.8 too."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-05-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: a plan at scale 1 : 600",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A rectangular garden measures 36 m by 24 m. It is drawn at scale 1 : 600. Find the plan dimensions in centimetres and the plan area."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Convert the real lengths to centimetres first."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "36\\text{ m}=3600\\text{ cm},\\qquad24\\text{ m}=2400\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Divide by 600 for the plan."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3600\\div600=6\\text{ cm},\\qquad2400\\div600=4\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{plan area}=6\\times4=24\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Check with the area factor 600²: the real area is 36 × 24 = 864 m² = 8 640 000 cm², and 8 640 000 ÷ 360 000 = 24 cm²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-05-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not use metres on one side of a ratio and centimetres on the other. A 1:600 scale is a linear ratio; area changes by 600². Label whether each quantity belongs to the plan, model or real object."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-25-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 1:100 plan shows a wall as 6 cm. Find the real wall length in metres."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "6",
        "canonical_latex": "6",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The real length is 600 cm, which is 6 m."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-05-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Shadows at the same time, plans, maps and models are similar figures. Write a proportion between corresponding measurements, or use the scale 1 : n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert to the same unit before using a scale, multiply by n for real lengths and divide by n for plan lengths, and use n² only for areas."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
